# T2 — what uid 0 inside `unshare -Ur` actually holds.
# Host uid is 1000; inside the namespace uid 0 maps to it.

## (a) identity and mapping inside the namespace
\$ unshare -Ur sh -c 'cat /proc/self/uid_map; id'
         0       1000          1
uid=0(root) gid=0(root) 组=0(root),65534(nobody)

## (b) can namespace-root read a host root-only file?
\$ cat /etc/shadow                  # as the host user
  READ_DENIED (EACCES)
\$ unshare -Ur cat /etc/shadow      # as namespace root
  READ_DENIED (EACCES)

## (c) can namespace-root read a file owned by a DIFFERENT uid?
# A second uid from /etc/subuid (host 100000) is mapped into a fresh
# namespace; it creates a 0600 file, then namespace-root tries to read it.
# Both processes belong to the same host user, so this isolates kernel
# ownership checks from the host user's own rights.
